{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\n`_reads._tracker` raises `store_uninitialized` only when the tracker directory is ABSENT\n(`src/rebar/_reads.py`, `if not os.path.isdir(tracker)`). A directory that EXISTS but is not a\nusable store — no `.git`, or a `.git` with no resolvable HEAD, or one mid-clone — satisfies\n`isdir`, so every read returns `[]`. The caller cannot distinguish \"this store is empty\" from\n\"this store is broken\", which is the exact confusion the guard was added to remove.\n\n## Encountered three times, each independently\n\n1. **In production.** The deployed MCP server served an EMPTY tracker for an extended period —\n   the store dir held `reviewbot-ensure-tickets` marker files and no `.git`; `isdir` was true so\n   the guard never fired and `rebar.list_tickets()` returned `READ_OK count=0`. Proven on the box.\n2. **`fathomable-yester-thrip` (change 2244, merged).** `clear_tracker_dir` empties CONTENTS but\n   leaves the directory, so between clear and a completed clone reads return `[]`. Recorded there\n   as a residual, not fixed.\n3. **`unfit-beneficial-whimbrel` AC3.** \"While the clone is in flight, tracker reads report\n   `store_uninitialized` rather than `[]`\" — NOT met, same reason; left unticked, points here.\n\n## Constraint the fix must respect\n\nAn EXISTING but empty INITIALIZED store must still read `[]` — a real contract with an existing\ntest (`tests/unit/test_reads_uninitialized_store.py`: \"An EXISTING but empty store is NOT an\nerror\"). So the discriminator cannot be emptiness. `.env-id` is ruled out as the read predicate\n(git-ignored, absent right after a valid clone; cf. `ab61-f3e8-6ee6-4905`).\n\n## Blast radius\n\n`_reads._tracker` funnels 7 read entry points, so this changes core read semantics repo-wide —\nthe reason it was scoped out of 2244.\n\n---\n\n## Fix plan (operator-APPROVED Option A; rebar-debug Phase 2)\n\n**Root cause (proven; full RCA in prior comment).** `_reads._tracker()` gates the read path on\n`os.path.isdir(tracker)` only; a dir that exists but has no `.git`, or `.git` with an unresolvable\nHEAD, satisfies `isdir`, so the guard never fires and reads yield `[]`. Attributed to\n`44fd-230c-5893-4246` (commit `84fc5f9b`).\n\n**Why a bare `.git`+HEAD predicate is WRONG (the escalation the operator resolved).** A pure\n`.git` present AND `git rev-parse --verify -q HEAD` predicate would reject a SUPPORTED production\nread path: `_snapshot.materialize_tickets` (`src/rebar/_snapshot/repo_snapshot.py`) checks the\ncommitted tickets tree out into a `.git`-LESS `.tickets-tracker/`, and the code-review gate agents\nread that snapshot in production (`LLMConfig.from_env` → `current_tickets_root()` →\n`_reads._tracker`). A `.git`+HEAD-only predicate raises `store_uninitialized` there and breaks the\ngate-agent read surface. So the predicate must accept a valid `.git`-less snapshot while still\nrejecting the production incident's marker-file-only directory.\n\n**Approved predicate (Option A — ONE shared content-based seam, no bypass flag).** A tracker dir\nis a usable store when `isdir(tracker)` AND EITHER:\n1. it has `.git` with a RESOLVABLE HEAD (`git rev-parse --verify -q HEAD`), OR\n2. it carries the rebar STORE STRUCTURE — a committed `.store-compat.json` record (committed into\n   the tickets tree, so it survives `materialize_tickets`' checkout and is present even in a\n   zero-event store), OR, as a legacy/fallback signal, ≥1 non-dot-prefixed subdirectory (a ticket\n   event dir — mirrors the reducer's enumeration in `reducer/_api.py`).\n\nThis is a deliberate read-SEMANTICS change (not behavior-preserving), scoped out of 2244 on\npurpose. Both the LIVE-store read path and the `.git`-less SNAPSHOT read path flow through the\nSAME predicate — no mode flag, no second code path — so read and write cannot diverge by\nconstruction.\n\n**Three cases it must distinguish (each test-pinned):**\n- **Production incident** — marker files only, no `.git`, no `.store-compat.json`, no event dirs\n  → RAISES `store_uninitialized` (the P1's core case).\n- **Empty-but-initialized LIVE store** — `.git` + resolvable HEAD → reads `[]` (existing contract\n  test, unchanged).\n- **Materialized snapshot** — `.git`-LESS pinned tree with committed `.store-compat.json` + event\n  dirs → READS (the gate-agent regression fix).\n\n**Operator edge ruling.** A store with ZERO events is VALID via the committed `.store-compat.json`\nrecord (an initialized store always carries it), so it reads `[]`. The predicate is NEVER keyed on\nemptiness.\n\n**Shared read+write predicate (safe for the write gate).** `event_prepare._ensure_initialized`\ncalls the SAME `store_is_usable`. Broadening it with clause 2 does not loosen the write gate in\npractice: writes only ever target the LIVE store, and git only populates the worktree after HEAD\nis set, so a live store carrying event dirs already has a resolvable HEAD (clause 1). A mid-clone\n(`.git`, no HEAD, no structure) is still rejected on both paths.\n\n**Write-path bounded-timeout (regression the operator flagged).** The HEAD probe MUST NOT add an\nunbounded git call to the write path. It reuses the store's existing bounded runner\n`gitutil.run_git_bounded(tracker, \"rev-parse\", \"--verify\", \"-q\", \"HEAD\", timeout=...)`, which folds\n`TimeoutExpired` into rc 124 and leaves all other errors (incl. a missing-git `OSError`) to\npropagate. `gitutil` is a stdlib-only leaf, so reusing it does not pull write-contention machinery\ninto the read path.\n\n**Design.** New stdlib-only leaf `src/rebar/_store/store_usability.py::store_is_usable(tracker)`\n= isdir AND (`_head_resolves()` OR `_carries_store_structure()`). `_head_resolves` probes via the\nbounded runner; a clean non-zero rc == \"HEAD unresolvable\"; an `OSError` (git binary missing) is a\nDISTINCT environment failure that PROPAGATES rather than collapsing into `store_uninitialized`\n(advisory T5b; test-pinned). `_carries_store_structure` = `.store-compat.json` present OR ≥1\nnon-dot subdirectory. The leaf keeps `_reads` out of the write-core import SCC.\n\n**Files.**\n- `src/rebar/_store/store_usability.py` (new) — shared content-based `store_is_usable`.\n- `src/rebar/_reads.py` — `_tracker` raises `store_uninitialized` when `not store_is_usable`.\n- `src/rebar/_store/event_prepare.py` — `_ensure_initialized` calls the shared predicate.\n- `src/rebar/audit/server.py` — reconcile the one consumer (docstring + log; behavior unchanged).\n- `tests/unit/test_reads_uninitialized_store.py` — read + snapshot + write-gate + predicate +\n  audit + mutation regression.\n\n**Caller-dependency gate + reconciliation.** The only consumer of `store_uninitialized` is\n`rebar.audit.server._audited_tickets`, a read-only WEB index whose correct failure mode is \"render\nan empty page, not 500\". Under the OLD read semantics a present-but-unusable store read `[]`, so it\nALSO rendered empty there — behavior unchanged by this fix. That module's docstring promised \"a\ngenuinely broken store is not silently blanked\", true only because broken stores did not raise. The\nfix makes them raise, so the index still degrades any `store_uninitialized` to empty (a web index\nmust not 500), the docstring is corrected, and the WARNING log is broadened so a present-but-unusable\nstore is LOGGED, not silently blanked. No caller depends on the old `[]`-for-broken-store on the\ndriving-agent read path (re-verified against the NEW predicate: the 7 `_tracker` entry points and\nthe `.git`-less snapshot path all behave correctly).\n\n**Acceptance criteria (each evidenced by a test in the named file).**\n- [x] READ: tracker dir exists but no `.git`/`.store-compat.json`/event dirs (marker files only)\n      → raises `store_uninitialized`, not `[]` (production incident).\n- [x] READ: mid-clone (`.git` present, HEAD unresolvable, no structure) → raises `store_uninitialized`.\n- [x] READ: existing empty INITIALIZED store (`.git`+HEAD) still reads `[]` (contract test unchanged).\n- [x] READ: valid clone with resolvable HEAD but NO `.env-id` still reads (reads ≠ writes on `.env-id`).\n- [x] READ (regression fix): `.git`-LESS materialized snapshot with committed `.store-compat.json`\n      + an event dir READS the ticket, not raises (gate-agent path).\n- [x] READ (operator edge): `.git`-less snapshot with `.store-compat.json` and ZERO events reads `[]`.\n- [x] WRITE: `_ensure_initialized` rejects a mid-clone store and still accepts an initialized store.\n- [x] WRITE: all `stage_and_commit` git calls (incl. the new HEAD probe) are timeout-BOUNDED\n      (`test_store_write_git_timeout_c2ba`).\n- [x] PREDICATE: `store_is_usable` is False for absent/no-`.git`/mid-clone/empty-dir, True for an\n      initialized store, a HEAD-resolvable clone without `.env-id`, a snapshot, an event-dir-only\n      legacy store, and an empty-but-committed snapshot.\n- [x] PREDICATE: a missing `git` binary (OSError) PROPAGATES rather than being reported as\n      `store_uninitialized` (T5b failure-taxonomy).\n- [x] AUDIT: `audit.server._audited_tickets` renders empty AND logs a warning for a\n      present-but-unusable store.\n- [x] Mutation guard: stripping the store-structure clause returns the `.git`-less snapshot read to\n      RAISING `store_uninitialized` (defect-seeded mutation check, sandboxed).\n\n**Verification.** RED→GREEN in the named test file; read + write + interface suites stay green;\n`make lint` (complexity ratchet new=0/increased=0) and `make typecheck` pass. The 8 pre-existing\nsandbox-git failures (`show-ref`/`ls-tree refs/heads/tickets` → 128) fail identically on clean\n`origin/main` and are environmental, not from this change.\n\n**Out of approved scope (parity — discovered tickets):** CLI read path (`reads_cli.py`) and other\nbare-`isdir` read sites — Step 7 parity sweep (`free-slothful-fennecfox`)."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787968519636354001,"uuid":"8eec9312-de73-403a-b324-c8c03bf88960"}